.TH KILL 2
.SH NAME
kill, sys_kill, raise \- send a signal to a process or a group (SYS_KILL)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long kill(int pid, int sig);
long sys_kill(int pid);          /* SIGKILL */
int raise(int sig);              /* to the caller itself */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kill
sends signal
.I sig
to process
.IR pid ,
or, if
.I pid
is negative, to every process of group
.RI - pid
(a shell's job,
.BR SPAWN_PGROUP ).
Signal 0 sends nothing: it only checks that it could be sent.
.PP
The signals:
.B SIGHUP
(1, the terminal went away),
.B SIGINT
(2, Ctrl-C on the terminal's foreground job),
.B SIGKILL
(9),
.B SIGUSR1
(10),
.B SIGUSR2
(12),
.B SIGTERM
(15); any number below
.B NSIG
(32) may be sent. Unless the process catches or ignores it
.RB ( signal (2)),
a signal ends the process at once, its exit status
.BR EXIT_SIGNAL ( sig );
.B SIGKILL
always does. An ignored one is dropped. A caught one goes to the thread
that set its function: if it waits (a message, a reply, a sleep, a
child), that call returns
.B EINTR
first.
.PP
Root may signal any process but the supervisor; others only their own
processes.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 if it went to at least one process, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I sig
is out of range, or
.I pid
is 0.
.TP
.B ESRCH
No such process or group.
.TP
.B EPERM
The process (every one of the group) is not the caller's to signal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR signal (2),
.BR tkill (2),
.BR sigwait (2),
.BR sys_wait (2),
.BR kill (1)
